---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import MovingOsContextCta from '../../components/MovingOsContextCta.astro';
import { en } from '../../i18n/en';

const title = 'How to Measure Furniture for Delivery | RoomFeng';
const description = 'Measure furniture, doorways, hallways, stair landings and elevator openings before delivery. Find the tightest point on the route before you buy or move it.';
const canonical = '/en/moving-furniture-size-check/';
const alternates = { zh: '/zh/moving-furniture-size-check/', en: '/en/moving-furniture-size-check/' };
const siteUrl = 'https://roomfeng.win';
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'How to Measure Furniture for Delivery Before You Buy or Move It',
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'RoomFeng' },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${siteUrl}${canonical}`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${siteUrl}/en/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Moving furniture size check', item: `${siteUrl}${canonical}` },
    ],
  },
];
const faq = [
  { q: 'What should I measure before moving furniture?', a: 'Measure the new room, the furniture, the room door, building entrance, elevator, stair turns, hallway width, and any tight landing or corner.' },
  { q: 'Is room fit enough to confirm moving fit?', a: 'No. A piece can fit in the destination room but fail on the route through doors, corridors, stairs, or elevators.' },
  { q: 'How do I handle furniture that can be disassembled?', a: 'Measure the largest disassembled part and the assembled footprint. Plan the room with the assembled size, then check the moving route with package or part sizes.' },
  { q: 'When should I decide not to move a piece?', a: 'If it blocks the new room, barely clears the route, costs more to move than replace, or cannot be disassembled safely, selling or donating may be smarter.' },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/en/room-layout-planner/', title: 'Room layout planner' },
  { href: '/en/furniture-fit-checker/', title: 'Furniture fit checker' },
  { href: '/en/apartment-furniture-planner/', title: 'Apartment furniture planner' },
  { href: '/en/bed-desk-wardrobe-layout/', title: 'Bed desk wardrobe layout' },
  { href: '/en/couch-fit-through-door-calculator/', title: 'Couch fit through door calculator' },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} lang="en" alternates={alternates} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container page-grid">
      <article class="card">
        <h1>How to measure furniture for delivery before you buy or move it</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          A furniture delivery check has two separate parts: the piece must fit inside the destination room, and it must pass every choke point on the route to that room. Measure the item in the form it will actually travel, then measure each door, hallway, turn, stair landing and elevator in order. The tightest point—not the final wall—is often what decides the move.
        </p>
        <p>
          Room fit alone does not answer delivery fit. A wardrobe can match a bedroom wall and still fail at a hallway turn. A sofa may need its legs removed. A boxed table, an assembled table, and a disassembled tabletop are different route problems, so write down which form each measurement describes.
        </p>

        <h2>Measure the item in the form it will actually travel</h2>
        <p>
          Ask the seller or mover for the dimensions of the delivered package when the piece arrives boxed. If the item is assembled before it moves, use the assembled dimensions. If legs, a base, cushions, or doors can be removed safely, record the largest rigid part after that change as a separate route check.
        </p>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measurement</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>assembled width × depth × height</td><td>Final room fit and operating space.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>packaged width × depth × height</td><td>Delivery route if the item arrives boxed.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>largest rigid part after disassembly</td><td>Route check when legs or a base can be removed.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>diagonal depth / relevant diagonal</td><td>A screening measurement when the item may need to tilt.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>removable feet, legs, cushions or doors</td><td>These may change the moving cross-section.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          A diagonal is a screening measurement, not a guarantee of a three-dimensional maneuver. Approach space, the angle of the turn, rigid protrusions, packaging, wall thickness, ceiling height, railings, and the flexibility of the item can change the result.
        </p>

        <h2>Walk the delivery route in order</h2>
        <p>
          Start outside the building and follow the same route a delivery team will use. Measure the clear opening, not only the frame size, and record anything that reduces usable clearance.
        </p>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Route checkpoint</th><th scope="col">Record</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>building/front entrance</td><td>clear width × height</td></tr>
              <tr><td>lobby or entry turn</td><td>available approach and turn space</td></tr>
              <tr><td>hallway</td><td>narrowest clear width + protrusions</td></tr>
              <tr><td>interior doorway</td><td>clear opening width × height</td></tr>
              <tr><td>stair run</td><td>usable width + headroom</td></tr>
              <tr><td>stair landing</td><td>width × depth + next-turn direction</td></tr>
              <tr><td>elevator door</td><td>clear opening width × height</td></tr>
              <tr><td>elevator cab</td><td>usable width × depth × height</td></tr>
              <tr><td>apartment door</td><td>clear width × height</td></tr>
              <tr><td>final room door</td><td>clear width × height</td></tr>
              <tr><td>destination room</td><td>final footprint + operating clearance</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Handles, trim, door closers, radiators, railings, low fixtures, wall décor, and packaging can reduce usable clearance. Include the route around a door, not just the opening: a rigid piece can clear the width and still lack enough approach or turning room.
        </p>

        <h2>Worked apartment example: a dining table with removable legs</h2>
        <p>This is an illustrative screening example, not a definitive route result.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Furniture: assembled dining table 72 × 38 × 30 in, with removable legs.</li>
          <li>After disassembly: tabletop 72 × 38 × 2 in.</li>
          <li>Route measurements: apartment door 34 × 80 in; elevator door 36 × 84 in; elevator cab 60 × 54 × 84 in.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Room fit alone does not answer delivery fit. The assembled footprint and the disassembled tabletop are different problems. The 2 in thick tabletop can potentially be presented edge-first to a 34 in doorway because its moving cross-section can be much smaller than the assembled footprint. That still does not prove the entire route works: a 72 in rigid tabletop needs enough approach, turning, and elevator-cab space. When a turn or rotation is close, confirm the actual geometry with the seller or mover.
        </p>

        <h2>Three different fit questions</h2>
        <h3>1. Room fit</h3>
        <p>Will the assembled piece physically fit in the final room? Use the <a href="/en/furniture-fit-calculator/">furniture fit calculator</a> for a quick footprint and requested-clearance check.</p>
        <h3>2. Everyday clearance</h3>
        <p>Can doors, drawers, chairs, and walkways still work after the piece is placed? Use the <a href="/en/furniture-fit-checker/">furniture fit checker</a> for the in-room clearance question.</p>
        <h3>3. Delivery-route fit</h3>
        <p>Can the item physically travel through the route? This page owns that route-planning intent. For a sofa and one doorway screening, use the <a href="/en/couch-fit-through-door-calculator/">couch fit through door calculator</a>, then return to the full route checklist for stairs, elevators, and turns.</p>

        <h2>Measurement references</h2>
        <p>These practical retailer guides describe furniture and delivery-path measurements. They are references, not law, building code, or engineering standards.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.cb2.com/customer-service/how-to-measure" rel="noopener noreferrer">CB2 — How to Measure</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.crateandbarrel.com/ideas-and-advice/how-to-measure-furniture" rel="noopener noreferrer">Crate &amp; Barrel — How to Measure Furniture for Delivery</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.thefurnituremart.com/inspiration/how-to-measure-furniture-for-delivery/" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Furniture Mart — How to Measure Furniture for Delivery</a></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to do this with the planner</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Open the <a href="/en/room-layout-planner/">room layout planner</a> and enter the destination room size.</li>
          <li>Add the room door and any windows or fixed storage that affects furniture placement.</li>
          <li>Add the furniture you plan to move using its assembled dimensions.</li>
          <li>Try final positions and check whether the item blocks the door, extends outside the room, or leaves a very narrow route.</li>
          <li>Export the plan, then separately compare the furniture dimensions with the actual moving route measurements.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If a large item barely fits in both the route and the room, decide before moving day whether it is worth the risk. Replacing one oversized piece can be cheaper and calmer than paying to move it twice.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <div>
          <h2>Check the new room before moving day</h2>
          <p>Plan the destination layout first, then verify doors, corridors, stairs, and elevators separately.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="button" href="/en/room-layout-planner/">{en.cta.openPlanner}</a>
      </div>
      <MovingOsContextCta locale="en" placement="en_moving_size" heading="Planning the whole move, not just one room?" copy="Moving & New Home OS combines measurements, furniture decisions, packing, budget and move-day planning in one private workspace." label="Plan My Move" />
      <AdSlot id="en-moving-mid" placement="en-moving-mid" />
      <Faq lang="en" items={faq} />
      <RelatedLinks lang="en" links={relatedLinks} />
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
